  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Some showers around into tonight before a \ntranquil stretch takes over.\n\nA closed low will continue to gradually shift eastward and exit\noff the New England coast this evening. The cyclonic flow \nremaining over our area along with lingering cool air aloft is \nresulting in quite a bit of cloud cover this afternoon. This \nlooks to continue for a while tonight before some thinning \nstarts to occur. More clearing may occur sooner across parts of \nDelmarva however. A shortwave will rotate across our area into \nthis evening and this will support some showers. While a few \nlightning strikes cannot be ruled out into this evening, there \nis currently meager MLCAPE and the visible satellite trends so \nfar are not all that supportive for thunderstorms. As a result, \nopted to remove thunder from the forecast especially given the \neven lower probability. The visible satellite imagery this \nafternoon also shows a weak low spinning southward off the New \nJersey coast. This feature may enhance the shower development \nsome along portions of the New Jersey coast. A northwesterly \nbreeze will ease into this evening. As the breeze lessens, it is\nnot out of the question that some patchy fog could develop \ngiven the residual ground moisture and some clearing of clouds, \nparticularly in sheltered areas. While confidence is not high in\nfog development, went ahead and included patchy fog with this \nforecast update given the potential for lowered visibilities in \nspots. \n\nSurface high pressure will then begin to settle in during \ntonight with some drier air. Some guidance hinting at a few \nrather isolated showers Friday afternoon, however given the \nclosed low/trough continuing to move away, opted to continue \nwith a dry forecast. Some warming aloft along with some increase\nin sunshine Friday will result in warmer afternoon \ntemperatures. Lighter flow however will result in a sea/bay \nbreeze developing by later in the afternoon. Depending on how \nfar inland this gets by early Friday evening, some increase in \nthe dew points should occur in its wake. Temperatures moderate \nsome more to start the weekend with high temperatures on \nSaturday right near average along with dry conditions. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A frontal system on Sunday and Monday will \nbring the next opportunity for widespread showers and \nthunderstorms.\n\nHigh pressure weakens late Saturday and is replaced by \nadvancing low pressure from the Ohio Valley area. This low \nstrengthens and moves west of our area later Sunday and into \nMonday. This system will bring increasing chances for showers \nand a few tstms Sunday afternoon for the western counties and \nall areas Sunday night and Monday. The PWAT values off the \nforecast soundings during these times show more 2+ inch PWAT \nvalues for our region, so it's possible that excessive rainfall \nmay develop for parts of the region. Latest outlook from WPC \nshows a marginal risk of excessive rainfall. Moisture and \ninstability will also increase ahead of the front, so we likely \nwill have to monitor the severe weather threat for the Sunday \nand Monday afternoon periods as well. However, our area is not \nin any long term severe outlooks, so there is a chance that \nthere are more hydro concerns with this system.",
